WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:06.440
Okay, so now what is exactly an inner join.

00:06.440 --> 00:11.240
So this type can return only the matching rows from both tables.

00:11.240 --> 00:14.680
So that means we will see in the output only matching rows.

00:14.680 --> 00:16.520
So now what do we need from the left table.

00:16.520 --> 00:18.560
We want only the matching data.

00:18.560 --> 00:21.040
So we will not get the whole circle of A.

00:21.080 --> 00:26.880
We will get only where we have an overlapping with the table B, so we want to see the data from A only

00:26.880 --> 00:32.640
if it exists in the table B and now what do we need from the table B exactly the same thing, only the

00:32.640 --> 00:33.400
matching data.

00:33.400 --> 00:38.760
So that means I don't want to see all the data from B, I want to see only the data and B that has a

00:38.760 --> 00:41.440
match from the table, A from the left side.

00:41.440 --> 00:45.520
And with that you will get only the matching data from both tables.

00:45.560 --> 00:47.720
Now let's see how we can write that in SQL.

00:47.720 --> 00:49.360
So it is usual query.

00:49.360 --> 00:51.240
And always we start with a select.

00:51.240 --> 00:53.760
So we select for example all the columns from.

00:53.760 --> 00:55.520
And here we specify the table name.

00:55.520 --> 00:56.560
So it's going to be a.

00:56.640 --> 00:57.840
So so far nothing new.

00:57.840 --> 01:01.080
But now we want to add as well the table B in the same query.

01:01.080 --> 01:04.100
In order to do that we use the keyword join.

01:04.100 --> 01:07.140
And then we say table be the name of the table.

01:07.180 --> 01:13.020
And since we have different types of joins in SQL, you can specify the type of the join before the

01:13.060 --> 01:13.940
keyword join.

01:13.940 --> 01:17.780
And if you don't specify anything, the default type is inner join.

01:17.780 --> 01:21.460
But my friends, the best practices is always mention the type.

01:21.460 --> 01:26.980
I don't like to skip the defaults because in projects maybe not everyone is aware of the defaults,

01:26.980 --> 01:27.940
so don't skip that.

01:27.940 --> 01:29.420
Always specify the type.

01:29.420 --> 01:30.180
So now what we're going to do.

01:30.180 --> 01:33.180
We're going to put the keyword inner before the join.

01:33.180 --> 01:37.340
And with that SQL gonna know how to deal with the rows between two tables.

01:37.340 --> 01:38.820
But still we are not done there.

01:38.860 --> 01:41.980
We have to tell SQL how to combine the tables.

01:41.980 --> 01:43.980
And with that we use the keyword on.

01:43.980 --> 01:47.220
And after that you specify the join condition.

01:47.220 --> 01:52.700
And as we learned in order to join two tables, we have to find out a common column in order to match

01:52.700 --> 01:53.180
the data.

01:53.180 --> 01:53.580
Right.

01:53.580 --> 01:56.500
And usually in SQL they are the keys or IDs.

01:56.500 --> 01:58.100
So the condition can be like this.

01:58.100 --> 02:03.020
The key from the table A must be equal to the key from the table B.

02:03.020 --> 02:04.860
So this is the join condition.

02:04.860 --> 02:10.680
And using this join scale can go and start matching the data from the left table and the right table.

02:10.680 --> 02:12.680
And there is one thing that is very important.

02:12.680 --> 02:17.800
While you are joining the tables, you have to understand about the order of the tables in your query.

02:17.840 --> 02:21.560
Now in the inner join, the order of the tables doesn't really matter.

02:21.560 --> 02:26.840
So whether you start from a or you start from B, it doesn't matter because you will get the same results.

02:26.840 --> 02:32.080
Both of the tables has the same priority and it doesn't matter where we start, whether we say from

02:32.120 --> 02:36.920
A join B or we say from B, join A, we will get the exact same results.

02:36.920 --> 02:40.760
So in the inner join you don't have to worry about the order of the tables.

02:40.760 --> 02:42.480
So that's all about the inner join.

02:42.520 --> 02:45.080
Now let's go back to SQL in order to practice okay.

02:45.120 --> 02:46.320
So now we have the following task.

02:46.360 --> 02:52.680
And it says all customers along with their orders but only for customers who have placed an order.

02:52.680 --> 02:58.160
So my friends that means we need the data from the customers and from the orders from two tables.

02:58.160 --> 03:00.120
And we have to put everything in one results.

03:00.120 --> 03:02.480
That means we have to join two tables.

03:02.520 --> 03:04.280
Now let's go and do it step by step.

03:04.280 --> 03:08.260
So we're going to go and say select star from Customers.

03:08.300 --> 03:10.300
And then we have to go and join it with the orders.

03:10.300 --> 03:12.020
We're going to say join orders.

03:12.060 --> 03:14.980
And now you have to go and specify the join type.

03:15.020 --> 03:17.260
Is it inner left full and so on.

03:17.260 --> 03:19.060
Well that's depend on the task.

03:19.100 --> 03:24.300
It says we want all customers but only for customers who have placed an order.

03:24.300 --> 03:26.180
So there is like condition right here.

03:26.180 --> 03:28.180
We don't want to see everything from the customer.

03:28.180 --> 03:34.940
We just want to see only the matching data, only if the customers has an order in the orders table.

03:34.940 --> 03:37.820
And for that we can go and use the inner join.

03:37.860 --> 03:40.340
Of course, if you can leave it like this, you will get the same effect.

03:40.340 --> 03:42.380
But I'm going to go and specify it like this.

03:42.420 --> 03:43.180
Inner join.

03:43.180 --> 03:47.820
Just to make it clear, we are speaking about the inner join, and after that we have to go and specify

03:47.820 --> 03:49.140
the join condition.

03:49.140 --> 03:52.900
So we have to go and find a common column between the customers and the orders.

03:52.900 --> 03:56.380
So how I usually do it, I go and explore both of the tables.

03:56.380 --> 04:03.140
So I'm going to go and select everything from customers and as well everything from the orders.

04:03.340 --> 04:04.860
So let's go and execute.

04:04.900 --> 04:06.060
Now we're going to start searching.

04:06.060 --> 04:09.220
Where do we have a common column between those two tables.

04:09.220 --> 04:14.520
So we have the from the first table, a first name country score, and you don't find any of those informations

04:14.520 --> 04:15.680
in the second table.

04:15.720 --> 04:20.440
The only one is the id, so the ID of the customer and the ID of the customer.

04:20.440 --> 04:22.280
You can find it in the orders.

04:22.320 --> 04:23.560
The second column here.

04:23.560 --> 04:26.320
So this is the common column between those two tables.

04:26.320 --> 04:31.240
And usually in databases we create IDs exactly for this in order to connect tables.

04:31.240 --> 04:35.720
So it's really rarely that we can use like a country or score or first name.

04:35.720 --> 04:38.720
In order to join tables we usually use the IDs.

04:38.720 --> 04:42.200
So let's go back to our query and use those two columns.

04:42.200 --> 04:46.920
So it's going to be the ID from the customers equal to the customer ID.

04:46.960 --> 04:47.560
So that's it.

04:47.560 --> 04:48.600
With that we have the condition.

04:48.600 --> 04:52.160
We have decided on the type and we can go and execute it.

04:52.160 --> 04:55.520
Now you can see we are getting only three customers, right?

04:55.560 --> 04:59.760
If you don't apply the inner join we can see that we have five customers.

04:59.760 --> 05:06.200
So that means actually we have two customers without any orders, any matching data from the other table.

05:06.240 --> 05:12.120
And as well you can see very nicely, we have now not only the columns from the customers, but as well

05:12.160 --> 05:14.820
all the columns from the orders side by side.

05:14.820 --> 05:18.380
So with that, we have combined the data and as well with that, we have solved the task.

05:18.380 --> 05:22.380
But we will not leave our query like this because it is not really good practices.

05:22.380 --> 05:27.300
What we have to do is to go and select only the columns that really make sense in our query, because

05:27.300 --> 05:31.140
in many cases in your tables you will have a lot of columns that is not needed.

05:31.140 --> 05:35.540
Like for example, if you check here, you see we have the customer ID here and as well the customer

05:35.540 --> 05:36.420
ID over here.

05:36.420 --> 05:39.580
So it's like repetition and it's enough to see it only once.

05:39.580 --> 05:43.100
So what you have to do is to go and pick few columns that you want.

05:43.140 --> 05:46.140
For example, I'm going to start with the ID, maybe the first name.

05:46.140 --> 05:48.060
And that's all from the first table.

05:48.060 --> 05:51.940
Let's go and get the order ID and I don't want the customer ID again.

05:51.940 --> 05:54.460
So from the second table I'll get as well the sales.

05:54.460 --> 05:56.100
So let's go and execute it.

05:56.100 --> 06:00.780
And with that you can see very nicely that customer's name and their orders with the sales.

06:00.820 --> 06:02.580
And now comes something very important.

06:02.580 --> 06:08.060
Sometimes if you have two tables you might have columns that having the same names like imagine the

06:08.060 --> 06:10.020
order ID in the table orders.

06:10.020 --> 06:10.980
It's called ID.

06:11.060 --> 06:13.980
So that means we have the same name in both tables.

06:14.020 --> 06:16.290
And this kind of makes Israel very confused.

06:16.290 --> 06:17.490
And here you will get an error.

06:17.530 --> 06:18.850
Tells you I really don't know.

06:18.850 --> 06:19.370
What do you mean?

06:19.370 --> 06:20.010
With the ID?

06:20.010 --> 06:22.850
Is it from the table customers or from the orders?

06:22.850 --> 06:24.810
So we have to tell SQL exactly.

06:24.810 --> 06:27.130
From which table did this column come from?

06:27.130 --> 06:32.890
So in SQL, in order to do that, what we do before the column name, you write again the table name

06:32.890 --> 06:35.170
the customers and then you make a dot.

06:35.210 --> 06:39.730
And now we are telling SQL this column the ID it comes from the table.

06:39.770 --> 06:42.610
Customers and SQL will not be confused about it.

06:42.610 --> 06:45.130
And it's going to go and get the ID from the customers.

06:45.130 --> 06:52.010
And for the second ID you can go over here and as well before it you say orders.id so that SQL knows

06:52.050 --> 06:56.130
okay, this ID come from the orders and the other one comes from the customers.

06:56.130 --> 07:01.170
And it is always good practice, especially if you are joining tables to always assign for each column

07:01.210 --> 07:01.690
a table.

07:01.690 --> 07:06.530
Because after a while, if you open your query and you see okay, the sales, does the sales come from

07:06.530 --> 07:08.370
the customers or the orders?

07:08.370 --> 07:11.690
And if you have a long list of columns, it's going to be really confusing.

07:11.690 --> 07:13.850
So that's why we consider it best practices.

07:13.850 --> 07:19.150
If you always assign for each column the table name, especially if you are doing joins.

07:19.150 --> 07:20.230
So it's going to be like this.

07:20.230 --> 07:24.870
But of course if you have like only one table, it's clear that all the columns and the select comes

07:24.870 --> 07:25.670
from this table.

07:25.710 --> 07:29.190
But since here we are dealing with multiple tables, it is good to show it like this.

07:29.230 --> 07:33.910
And of course here we don't have the ID, we have the order ID and the same thing for the join condition.

07:33.910 --> 07:39.510
So the ID from here comes from the customers and the customer ID come from the orders.

07:40.070 --> 07:43.750
So now it is clear for everyone which column come from which table.

07:43.750 --> 07:44.870
But now you might say, you know what.

07:44.910 --> 07:46.870
Each time I have to write the customers.

07:46.870 --> 07:52.830
This is very long name and sometimes in real projects you can see tables that has really long name and

07:52.830 --> 07:55.910
it's going to be really annoying to add it each time before each column.

07:55.910 --> 07:56.350
Right?

07:56.350 --> 08:01.750
So instead of that we can go and assign aliases for the tables, not only for the columns.

08:01.750 --> 08:06.910
So usually we go over here and say as and maybe you can go and use only one character like the first

08:06.910 --> 08:07.870
character C.

08:07.910 --> 08:12.670
And now instead of saying customers, you can go over here and say, see the same thing for the second

08:12.670 --> 08:18.070
column and as well over here, and you can use now the C in everywhere in your query, the same thing

08:18.070 --> 08:18.670
for the orders.

08:18.670 --> 08:20.530
You can go over here and say us all.

08:20.570 --> 08:22.490
And now, instead of orders, you say.

08:22.530 --> 08:22.850
Oh.

08:24.970 --> 08:29.890
And here and now it is very easy to see those two columns comes from the sea.

08:29.930 --> 08:32.770
That means the customers and those two columns comes from the.

08:32.770 --> 08:33.890
Oh, the orders.

08:33.890 --> 08:37.410
Those are the best practices as you are joining tables together in SQL.

08:37.450 --> 08:39.250
And of course, with that we have solved the task.

08:39.250 --> 08:42.290
And about the order of the tables, it doesn't matter where do you start?

08:42.290 --> 08:48.330
So for example, if you take the orders here and put it in the join and get the orders in the from,

08:48.330 --> 08:52.690
so I just switch the tables and execute it, you will get the exact same results.

08:52.690 --> 08:56.730
So if you are doing inner join between two tables, don't worry about the order of the tables.

08:56.770 --> 09:01.650
Okay, so now let's go and understand exactly how SQL executed the inner join okay.

09:01.690 --> 09:03.690
So now again here we have our query.

09:03.690 --> 09:06.250
Then we have the two tables customers and orders.

09:06.290 --> 09:08.970
And here we have the ID where we are joining the data.

09:08.970 --> 09:11.170
So this is the ID from the table customers.

09:11.170 --> 09:13.850
And this is the customer ID that we have in the orders.

09:13.890 --> 09:15.930
Now let's see how SQL can execute this.

09:15.930 --> 09:19.010
So we are seeing I would like to see the ID and the first name.

09:19.010 --> 09:23.190
So we will get the ID the first name from the table Customers.

09:23.190 --> 09:28.750
And we would like to get the order ID and as well the sales from the table orders.

09:28.750 --> 09:31.270
So our result can focus on those four columns.

09:31.310 --> 09:35.510
Now the data should be joined between those two tables using the inner join and SQL.

09:35.550 --> 09:40.070
Gonna start from the left table from the customers because we say from customers.

09:40.070 --> 09:44.310
So its gonna start matching the ID from the left table with the right table.

09:44.310 --> 09:48.710
So it's gonna say okay, is there a match from the first record from the first order?

09:48.750 --> 09:54.710
Well, yes, it is the same ID and then it's gonna say, okay, that condition is fulfilled and we are

09:54.710 --> 09:55.950
allowed to see the data.

09:55.950 --> 09:59.270
So the data will be presented in the output.

09:59.270 --> 10:05.510
So we can have the ID Maria and the order ID from Maria and the sales of this order.

10:05.550 --> 10:06.670
So there is a match.

10:06.670 --> 10:08.830
Then it's going to go to the second record.

10:08.830 --> 10:13.510
Well we don't have a match, the third we don't have much and so on for the last one.

10:13.510 --> 10:19.030
So we have only one match for this ID, then SQL gonna go again to the customers and pick the second

10:19.030 --> 10:21.870
one and start matching again with the first order.

10:21.870 --> 10:22.710
Do we have a match?

10:22.710 --> 10:23.310
We'll know.

10:23.350 --> 10:24.890
Then it's gonna go to the second.

10:24.930 --> 10:26.450
Well, now we have a match.

10:26.450 --> 10:27.810
So SQL going to be happy.

10:27.810 --> 10:30.930
The condition is fulfilled and we will see the results.

10:30.930 --> 10:32.370
So we're going to see the first name.

10:32.370 --> 10:36.170
And as well the order information for this customer in the output.

10:36.210 --> 10:37.570
It's going to keep searching.

10:37.570 --> 10:39.610
So we don't have a match as well here.

10:39.610 --> 10:40.570
So that's it.

10:40.610 --> 10:42.530
Now for the third customer as well.

10:42.530 --> 10:46.170
From the start they are match now to the second to the third.

10:46.170 --> 10:47.090
And here we have a match.

10:47.090 --> 10:51.210
So SQL going to go and show this information since there is a match.

10:51.210 --> 10:58.010
So the customer three George with the order from this customer order ID and the sales as well in the

10:58.010 --> 11:00.970
output, now it's going to go and keep continuing the search.

11:00.970 --> 11:02.530
Well we don't have any match.

11:02.530 --> 11:05.850
Then it's going to go to the fourth customer and start matching.

11:05.850 --> 11:07.050
Do we have here an ID.

11:07.090 --> 11:08.290
Do we have here a match?

11:08.290 --> 11:08.970
Well no.

11:09.010 --> 11:11.170
Then the second, third and fourth.

11:11.170 --> 11:14.850
We don't have any order for this ID there is no match at all.

11:14.890 --> 11:21.010
And since we are saying inner join, then SQL will not allow to show the data of this customer in the

11:21.010 --> 11:21.570
results.

11:21.570 --> 11:24.930
There is no match and SQL going to totally ignore this customer.

11:24.970 --> 11:29.630
Then we're going to go to the last one and start as well, matching this ID with the orders.

11:29.630 --> 11:31.510
Well, there is no match as well.

11:31.550 --> 11:34.710
SQL going to go and exclude this user from the results.

11:34.710 --> 11:37.350
So this is exactly how the inner join works.

11:37.350 --> 11:42.590
It starts from the left side and start matching the data on the right side, and only if there is match,

11:42.590 --> 11:44.630
the result is going to be presented in the output.

11:44.630 --> 11:48.670
And this is exactly why we are getting this results and how the inner join works.

11:48.670 --> 11:53.430
So now if you look again to the reasons why we are joining tables, we can say we can use the inner

11:53.430 --> 11:58.310
join in order to recombine the multiple tables into one big picture.

11:58.310 --> 12:02.990
So the first use case and as well we can use the inner join in order to filter the data.

12:02.990 --> 12:07.630
So since we are saying only the matching data, that means we are filtering the data.

12:07.630 --> 12:11.190
We are checking the existence of the records in another table.

12:11.190 --> 12:16.670
So you can use inner join either to combine data from multiple tables, or you can use it as well only

12:16.670 --> 12:20.430
for filtering purposes only to check the existence of your rows.

12:20.430 --> 12:22.630
So this is usually the two use cases of inner.

12:22.670 --> 12:23.030
All right.

12:23.030 --> 12:25.470
So that's all about the first type the inner join.

12:25.510 --> 12:27.870
Next we are going to talk about the left join.

12:27.870 --> 12:29.950
So we're going to focus on the left side.

12:29.950 --> 12:30.470
So let's go.
